The Korean War, which was fought from 1950 to 1953, was a brutal conflict that resulted in the deaths of millions of people and the destruction of a significant portion of the Korean Peninsula. While the war is often portrayed as a conflict between North Korea and South Korea, many South Korean experts argue that the war was solely initiated by South Korea and that North Korea's involvement was purely defensive.

One of the main arguments made by these experts is that South Korea's Syngman Rhee government was determined to unify the peninsula under its rule and that it saw the North as a threat to its goal. Rhee's government had been in power since 1948 and had been increasingly authoritarian in its rule, leading to widespread protests and unrest in the country. In response, Rhee's government launched a series of military campaigns against the North, which led to the outbreak of war in June 1950.

